# mill over the corpus's shared atoms, written by phil/corpus from readings/mill/logic: not to be edited
atom	universal	my maxim is a universal law: everyone in difficulty may promise whatever he pleases with the purpose of not keeping it
atom	nobody_believes	no one would consider that anything was promised to him, but would ridicule all such statements as vain pretences
atom	promise_possible	the promise itself, and the end one has in view in it, is possible
atom	holds	the maxim holds as a universal law of nature without contradicting itself
atom	mill_principle_ground	the rule 'So act, that the rule on which thou actest would admit of being adopted as a law by all rational beings' is the universal first principle, the origin and ground of moral obligation
atom	mill_shows_contradiction	Kant shows that there would be a contradiction, a logical or physical impossibility, in the adoption by all rational beings of the most outrageously immoral rules of conduct
atom	mill_shows_bad_consequences	Kant shows that the consequences of the universal adoption of such rules would be such as no one would choose to incur
atom	mill_deduces_duties	Kant succeeds in deducing the actual duties of morality from this precept
P1	S2	mill_principle_ground	says kant
P2	S3	¬mill_shows_contradiction	kind empirical
P3	S4	mill_shows_bad_consequences ∧ ¬mill_shows_contradiction	kind empirical
A1	-	mill_deduces_duties → mill_shows_contradiction
C	S3	¬mill_deduces_duties	from P2 A1
